        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_57.lnote.oe.42" n="42" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">114</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">onbyte <hi rend="italic">So</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">onbyte So</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_57.lnote.oe.43" n="43" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">114</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">ðæne <hi rend="italic">fügt zu Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">ðæne added by Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_57.lnote.oe.44" n="44" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">114</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">þryd. <hi rend="italic">So</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">þryd. So</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Ine_57.lnote.oe.45" n="45" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">114</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">dæle ðære æhta <hi rend="italic">Ld</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">dæle ðære æhta Ld</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_57.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">78</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">H modernisiert (wie B in Af 5, 1) und Q missversteht <hi rend="italic">ærn</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">H modernises (as does B in Af 5, 1) and Q misunderstands <hi rend="italic">ærn</hi></seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_57.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">78</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">hine,</hi> wie So liest, lautet am archaischsten und datiert jedenfalls vór 12. Jh.; <hi rend="italic">hit</hi> kann aus 11. Jh. stammen. Schwerlich fehlte es im Or. mit Ines Absicht, wahrscheinlich aber schon im Archetyp. — Die Ertappung des Gestohlenen gilt gleich Handhaftigkeit; <hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 3</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">hine,</hi> as So reads, is the most archaic form and at any rate dates from before the 12th cent.; <hi rend="italic">hit</hi> may come from the 11th cent. It was hardly missing from the orig. by Ine’s intention, but it was probably already missing in the archetype. — The discovery of the stolen goods counts as equivalent to being caught in the act; <hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 3</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_57.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">78</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Das Wort wird regelmässig von Späteren modernisiert; <hi rend="italic">s. Wb (un)synnig, -ngian.</hi> — Der Besitzer kommt nicht zur Leugnung und verwirkt friedlos (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 10 b ff.) Leben und Vermögen</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The word is regularly modernised by later writers; <hi rend="italic">see Wb (un)synnig, -ngian.</hi> — The owner is not allowed to deny the charge and, as an outlaw (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 10 b ff.), forfeits life and property</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_57.lnote.de.4" n="4" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">78</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Über diese Phrase s. Toller <hi rend="italic">Suppl.</hi> s. v. <hi rend="italic">án</hi> IX</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">On this phrase see Toller <hi rend="italic">Suppl.</hi> s. v. <hi rend="italic">án</hi> IX</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_57.lnote.de.5" n="5" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">78</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">H modernisiert</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">H modernises</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_57.lnote.de.6" n="6" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">78</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Ehefrau 9—9 b</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Ehefrau 9—9 b</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_57.lnote.de.7" n="7" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">78</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Artikel B’s moderner</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">B’s article is more modern</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_57.lnote.de.8" n="8" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">78</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">S. Gl</hi> Eheliches Güterrecht 2. 4</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">See Gl</hi> Eheliches Güterrecht 2. 4</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_57.lnote.de.9" n="9" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">78</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">H. B modernisieren</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">H. B modernise</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Ine_57.lnote.de.10" n="10" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">78</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">B’s Ergänzung; <hi rend="italic">sceatt</hi> ist archaisch in diesem Sinne; <hi rend="italic">vgl. Wb</hi> 2 und ¹/₃ <hi rend="italic">sceatt</hi> Abt 78 f.</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">B’s addition; <hi rend="italic">sceatt</hi> is archaic in this sense; <hi rend="italic">cf. Wb</hi> 2 and ¹/₃ <hi rend="italic">sceatt</hi> Abt 78 f.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Ine_57.attnote.fn.24" n="24" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">54</biblScope></bibl><label>ærne</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">huse</hi> (<hi rend="italic">vel ærne</hi>). H*.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Ine_57.attnote.fn.25" n="25" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">54</biblScope></bibl><label>⁊</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">⁊ <hi rend="italic">hit</hi>. H. <hi rend="italic">hitt</hi>. B*.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Ine_57.attnote.fn.26" n="26" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">54</biblScope></bibl><label>synnig</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">scyldig</hi>. H, So &amp; Ld.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Ine_57.attnote.fn.1" n="1" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">56</biblScope></bibl><label>ealdore</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">hlaforde</hi>. H.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Ine_57.attnote.fn.2" n="2" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">56</biblScope></bibl><label>sceat</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">gescead</hi>. H. <hi rend="italic">ðæne ðriddan dæl ðære æhta</hi>. B.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="Ine_57.attnote.n.1" n="1" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">191</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">It would seem that two-thirds of the property—that is all except the one-third which could be claimed by the wife—was to be forfeited. See Liebermann, <hi rend="italic">Glossar</hi>, <hi rend="italic">s.v.</hi> ‘eheliches Güterrecht,’ 2—4; Pollock and Maitland, <hi rend="italic">History of English Law</hi>, <hi rend="smallcaps">ii</hi>. p. 362 ff.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="translationFootnote" xml:id="Ine_57.whinote.n.6" n="6" source="#whitelock"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">406</biblScope></bibl><label>portion</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">i.e. if his possessions arc forfeit, a third is retained by her.</seg></note>
